{"name":"Tin Soldier","hex":"#bebebe","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"tin soldier","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A toy soldier made out of tin.","One who only plays at being a soldier."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tin_soldier"}],"entries":[{"title":"Tin soldier","blocks":[{"text":"Tin soldiers are miniature toy soldiers that are very popular in the world of collecting. They can be bought finished or in a raw state to be hand-painted. They are generally made of pewter, tin, lead, other metals or plastic. Often very elaborate scale models of battle scenes, known as dioramas, are created for their display.\nTin soldiers were originally almost two-dimensional figures, often called \"little Eilerts\" or \"flats\". They were the first toy soldiers to be mass-produced. Though largely superseded in popularity from the late 19th century by fully rounded three-dimensional lead figures, these flat tin soldiers continue to be produced."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Box_set_military_band_toy_soldiers_%2826395354922%29.jpg/330px-Box_set_military_band_toy_soldiers_%2826395354922%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tin_soldier"},{"title":"Tin Soldier (film)","blocks":[{"text":"Tin Soldier is a 2025 American action thriller film directed by Brad Furman, written by Furman, Jess Fuerst, and Pablo Fenjves. It stars Scott Eastwood, John Leguizamo, Nora Arnezeder, Jamie Foxx, and Robert De Niro. Filmed in 2022, it was released in 2025, and was overwhelmingly disliked by critics."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/1/12/Tin_Soldier_film_poster.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tin_Soldier_(film)"},{"title":"Tin","blocks":[{"text":"Tin is a chemical element; it has the symbol Sn and atomic number 50. A metallic-gray metal, tin is soft enough to be cut with little force, and a bar of tin can be bent by hand with little effort. When bent, a bar of tin makes a sound, the so-called \"tin cry\", as a result of twinning in tin crystals."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Sn-Alpha-Beta.jpg/330px-Sn-Alpha-Beta.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tin"},{"title":"Soldier","blocks":[{"text":"A soldier is a person who is a member of an army. A soldier can be a conscripted or volunteer enlisted person, a non-commissioned officer, a warrant officer, or an officer."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/2ID_Recon_Baghdad.jpg/330px-2ID_Recon_Baghdad.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soldier"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Castingtinsoldiers.jpg/500px-Castingtinsoldiers.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Castingtinsoldiers.jpg/500px-Castingtinsoldiers.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Tin soldiers being cast in German molds from the early 20th century. The two mold halves are clamped together, and the metal (an alloy of tin and lead, heated to approx. 300 °C) is poured into the mold. When the metal has solidified, the mold is cracked open. Sprues (pouring channels) and extraneous flash (metal that has penetrated cracks and air channels in the mold) are seen in the third image, and have been removed from the castings in the last image.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACastingtinsoldiers.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Vilhelm_Pedersen%2C_DK2-0006%2C_ubt.jpeg/500px-Vilhelm_Pedersen%2C_DK2-0006%2C_ubt.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Vilhelm_Pedersen%2C_DK2-0006%2C_ubt.jpeg/1280px-Vilhelm_Pedersen%2C_DK2-0006%2C_ubt.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Vilhelm_Pedersen%2C_DK2-0006%2C_ubt.jpeg/500px-Vilhelm_Pedersen%2C_DK2-0006%2C_ubt.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Illustration by Vilhelm Pedersen for The Steadfast Tin Soldier (Den Standhaftige Tinsoldat).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVilhelm_Pedersen%2C_DK2-0006%2C_ubt.jpeg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Tin-2.jpg/500px-Tin-2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Tin-2.jpg/500px-Tin-2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Solidified droplet of molten tin","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATin-2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Sword_bronze_age_%282nd_version%29.jpg/500px-Sword_bronze_age_%282nd_version%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Sword_bronze_age_%282nd_version%29.jpg/500px-Sword_bronze_age_%282nd_version%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Ceremonial giant bronze dirk of the Plougrescant-Ommerschans type, Plougrescant, France, 1500–1300 BC","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASword_bronze_age_(2nd_version).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Tin%28II%29-chloride-xtal-1996-3D-balls-front.png/500px-Tin%28II%29-chloride-xtal-1996-3D-balls-front.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Tin%28II%29-chloride-xtal-1996-3D-balls-front.png/1280px-Tin%28II%29-chloride-xtal-1996-3D-balls-front.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Tin%28II%29-chloride-xtal-1996-3D-balls-front.png/500px-Tin%28II%29-chloride-xtal-1996-3D-balls-front.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Ball-and-stick models of the structure of solid stannous chloride (SnCl2)[56]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATin(II)-chloride-xtal-1996-3D-balls-front.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Cassiterite09.jpg/500px-Cassiterite09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Cassiterite09.jpg/500px-Cassiterite09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sample of cassiterite, the main ore of tin","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACassiterite09.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/SnPrice.png/500px-SnPrice.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/SnPrice.png/1280px-SnPrice.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/SnPrice.png/500px-SnPrice.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"World production and price (US exchange) of tin","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASnPrice.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Tin_Prices.webp/500px-Tin_Prices.webp?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Tin_Prices.webp/1280px-Tin_Prices.webp?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Tin_Prices.webp/500px-Tin_Prices.webp?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Tin prices from 2008 to 2022  See also: 2020s commodities boom","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATin_Prices.webp"}],"sounds":{}}}